%% First-tick init (controller starts at rest)
if isempty(xK)
    xK = zeros(size(Ad, 1), 1);   % nK x 1; size(Ad,1) is constant-folded for codegen
end

%% Discrete dynamic output feedback
e  = xd - state;        % 6x1 tracking error [X Y Z phi theta psi]
ud = Cd * xK + Dd * e;  % 4x1 command: thrust-DELTA + moments (output uses current state)
xK = Ad * xK + Bd * e;  % advance controller state (Tustin update)

%% Hover trim -> absolute thrust, then shared saturation clip
% Channel 1 of ud is thrust DELTA from hover; add m*g (controller-side mass, so
% mass-uncertainty injection flows through here as in the dSMC).
u = ud + [constants.m_uncertain * constants.g; 0; 0; 0];

% Anti-windup: DELIBERATELY NONE (design decision, not an oversight). The clip
% below can saturate while the pseudo-integrator controller states in xK keep
% integrating the tracking error, so aggressive ballistic deploys may wind up
% and overshoot. That is the intended NAIVE-baseline handicap -- adding
% back-calculation/conditioning anti-windup here would blur the fair-comparison
% contrast with the dSMC, whose Plan A+ auxiliary-state anti-windup is meant to
% be its distinguishing edge. The eps1 (=1e-4) integrator LEAK in hinf_design.m's
% W1 has a leak time constant ~1e4 s, far longer than a 30 s trial, so it does
% NOT meaningfully bound the windup within a trial. If a bounded-command H-inf
% variant is ever wanted, condition a leak/clamp on xK on the apply_rotor_clip
% saturation flag -- but keep it OUT of the fair-peer arm.
u = apply_rotor_clip(u, constants);   % identical actuator limits as every arm

end
